An often overlooked consequence of the emerging era of smart dust enabled by Ultra-low Size, Weight, and Power (UlSWaP) Internet-of-Things devices is managing software complexity. UlSWaP devices help solve many important societal problems across disparate domains, but this requires software support. Managing the demands of software complexity forces smart dust vendors to turn to third-party software libraries that solve common concerns. This opens the door to a new problem, where third-party library vendors must protect their Intellectual Property and secret data (e.g., keys) from end-users and competitors alike. Device vendors attempt to address this security need through the addition of on-chip Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) in their latest UlSWaP chips. While these TEEs successfully protect against untrusted software, they fail to protect against attacks that coopt trusted software as an indirect means of attack: Confused Deputy attacks. We arrest the Confused Deputy undermining UlSWaP TEEs through a lightweight redesign of deployed UlSWaP TEEs. Our TEE Sheriff employs hardware/software co-design to achieve “just right” hardware support that provides a secure environment for trusted software—even from itself—without a large increase in hardware size or complexity, while maintaining software expressiveness. At the core of Sheriff is the creation of mandated introspection points at all (i.e., both expected and unexpected) control flows to and from the secure memory zone. This ensures that all data and control flows are sanitized between zones, eliminating the ability for the attacker to coopt trusted software. We implement Sheriff in a TI MSP430 processor to validate its security, measure its performance, and facilitate industry adoption. Our evaluation using real-world programs shows that Sheriff prevents Confused Deputy attacks while also maintaining security against direct attacks from untrusted software, all while incurring <1% hardware overhead and <3% software run-time overhead.